#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{4}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $3{x}^{2}-xz=6-3xyz$ at the point $(1,0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338941 :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)$, find $∇h(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338942 :question/problem "Find $∇g(x,y,z)$ given that  $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338952 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=(2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{xy},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}((2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2xy+{y}^{2}}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(-\\frac{x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x+y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{x+y}{x}+\\frac{2x+y}{x})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(-\\frac{x+y}{y}+\\frac{x+2y}{y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338958 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338959 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|=3\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4})^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7)^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339074 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)]+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}) & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339075 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x{e}^{y}={x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ [(x)({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(1)({e}^{y})] & =2x \\\\ x\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x \\\\ x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339079 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y+4y=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+4y & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x]+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4) & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339080 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $2xy+4{y}^{3}={x}^{2}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy+4{y}^{3} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy+4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ [2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y]+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2x+12{y}^{2}) & =2x-2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-2y}{2x+12{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{x+6{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339081 :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $2{x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+6) \\\\ [2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 6{x}^{2}]+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{2}y+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+4y) & =8x-6{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-6{x}^{2}y}{2{x}^{3}+4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339099 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}+y)\\,i+(2y{x}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2(1)(2)^{2}+(2))\\,i+(2(2)(1)^{2}+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339107 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2xy+9.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110 :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2xy={x}^{2}+8$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy & ={x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=8.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ (2(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -2(y-x) & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x)^{2}+2x(x)-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=2$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=-2$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339115 :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+2xy=-5y+4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339122 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy=1-3x.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy & =1-3x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,1)={f}_{y}(1,1)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,1)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)j \\\\  & =(4y{x}^{3}-4x)i-(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4y+3)j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(4(1)(1)^{3}-4(1))i-(6(1)^{2}(1)^{2}-4(1)+3)j \\\\  & =-5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341841 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d45a9c753270dfd9186bc16736d1815a11997c88d2f35a0c40f487e6acef66c6.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-2,3)$ and the point $X(1,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-2))}^{2}+{(-1-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341842 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2ff035f38f5892f61d1eb037c7e39c59c8ec5104babac45224c4902152a0cfaa.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-1,-3)$ and the point $X(1,1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-1))}^{2}+{(1-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{20})^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341843 :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e68ede9db2528ea607fc6fd63f0ec4ca1c1378982239913ef49153e1cf5b609.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(3,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,2)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(2-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{41})^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341845 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x) & =\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341846 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}x+\\operatorname{sec}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}=\\operatorname{tan}x+\\operatorname{sec}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341847 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341858 :question/problem "![](images/f5/f53996727138b9442ac041cf492618db8287b54ba652c4f9e375cdde27dc1862.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & \\approx 53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341859 :question/problem "![](images/36/36af7324fd2112d94feddc5a1a02a91d97bbc0866cb93a6ca711dd73f566305a.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.658,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & =2{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341925 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{64}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341976 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,-3,2⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,-1,-1⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-3,0⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-3,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341977 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,4,3⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,3,2⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-1,4⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =14\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨14,8,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-2,4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨14,8,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 14+4\\cdot 8+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341978 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-2,-1,0⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,1,4⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-2,0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,8,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341979 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,-2,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,-2,4⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{q},$ $\\mathbf{r},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2((-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =12-11 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,1,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,-2,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{q},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341983 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-1,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨4,-2,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨4,-2,6⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12+2+12 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342034 :question/problem "![](images/86/8637901efc126236b190e7012cf65a06be078cb21c3093eff39b927493000138.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1.5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342036 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t-2}+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{3}+2,\\,t\\ge 2,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{t-2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, $x=\\sqrt{t-2}+1\\ge 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that ${t}^{3}\\ge 8$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, $y={t}^{3}+2\\ge 8+2=10.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342038 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-2 \\\\ -2 & \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ 1-2 & \\le 1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1+2 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2-3 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le 2-3 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342039 :question/problem "![](images/37/377a871e9328e0a8b3c12d8904ecc16a2e6e3a17fa7dba05925bb6f2e5d7e4ad.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342042 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2+1 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 2+1 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\le \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$"}]}
